.\" xsnip — screenshot tool with a minimal markup editor
.TH XSNIP 1 "2026-11-02" "xsnip 3.1" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
xsnip \- take a screenshot of the screen or a region and mark it up
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B xsnip
.RB [ \-s ]
.RB [ \-o
.IR file ]
.RB [ \-d
.IR seconds ]
.RB [ \-a ]
.RB [ \-\-colors
.IR c1,c2,c3,c4,c5 ]
.RB [ \-\-color
.IR N|#rrggbb ]
.RB [ \-\-pen
.IR percent ]
.RB [ \-\-text
.IR percent ]
.RB [ \-\-window
.IR dialog|normal ]
.RB [ \-\-pos
.IR center|tl|tr|bl|br ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B xsnip
grabs the screen (or a rectangle selected with the mouse), opens a small
window with the image and a minimal set of tools: a pen and a text tool.
Clicking the checkmark button sends the result to the clipboard or writes it
to a file.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-s, \-\-select
Select a region with the mouse. Esc or a rectangle smaller than 5x5 cancels
the shot (exit status 1).
.TP
.B \-a, \-\-all
Grab the whole virtual screen (all monitors). By default only the screen
under the pointer is grabbed, and after
.B \-s
the screen where the selection was made.
.TP
.BI \-o,\ \-\-output\  file
Write a PNG to
.I file
instead of copying it to the clipboard.
.TP
.BI \-d,\ \-\-delay\  seconds
Wait before taking the shot. Fractions are allowed.
.TP
.BI \-\-colors\  c1,c2,c3,c4,c5
Palette of five colours as
.BR #rrggbb .
The default is red, green, yellow, white, black.
.TP
.BI \-\-color\  N|#rrggbb
Active colour: an index in the palette (starting at one) or a
.B #rrggbb
value.
.TP
.BI \-\-pen\  percent
Pen width as a percentage of
.IR min(width,\ height)
of the image, from 0.1 to 5 (default 1).
.TP
.BI \-\-text\  percent
Text size in the same percentage, from 1 to 50 (default 10).
.TP
.BI \-\-window\  dialog|normal
Window type of the editor.
.B dialog
(the default) marks it as a dialog, so a tiling window manager such as i3
keeps it floating and honours
.BR \-\-pos .
.B normal
gives a regular window that the window manager tiles.
.TP
.BI \-\-pos\  center|tl|tr|bl|br
Position of the editor window: centre or a corner (top-left, top-right,
bottom-left, bottom-right) with a 10-point margin, counted from the monitor
the window opens on.
.TP
.B \-h, \-\-help
Print a short help message.
.SH THE EDITOR
The toolbar is scaled from the tallest monitor (800 points of height is the
base). From the left: the undo button (a counter-clockwise arrow), the tools
"pen", "text" and "crop", five colour swatches, the buttons that make the
tool smaller and bigger, and on the right the clipboard and the checkmark
("done") buttons. Hovering over a button shows a hint with its name and the
keyboard shortcut.
.PP
.nf
p, 1         pen
t, 2         text
r, 3         crop
4 ... 8      colour from the palette
Ctrl+Z       undo the last stroke, text or crop
Enter        place the typed text; outside text input it is the "done" button
Esc          cancel text input, otherwise close the window
.fi
.PP
The "crop" tool: drag a rectangle inside the editor (or set two corners with
two clicks), and the image is cut down to it. The annotations move with the
image, and the pen is selected again. Cropping goes on the same undo stack as
the strokes.
.PP
Sizes are given in percent of
.IR min(width,\ height)
of the image, so they look the same on 800x600 and on 4K: the pen runs from
0.1 to 5 percent (0.1 step, default 1), the text from 1 to 50 percent (0.5
step, default 10).
.PP
Text is typed through XIM, so Cyrillic and Chinese work as in any other X11
application. While typing, a caret as tall as the chosen text size shows where
the text will start, and the input line at the bottom is drawn with a font of
"tallest monitor height / 25".
.SH LANGUAGE
The interface, the tooltips and the help are translated into English (the
default), Russian and Chinese. The language is taken from the environment:
.BR XSNIP_LANG ,
then
.BR LC_ALL ,
.BR LC_MESSAGES ,
.BR LANG
(the first two letters matter: ru, zh).
.SH FILES
.TP
.I $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/xsnip/config
Saved settings (usually
.IR ~/.config/xsnip/config ):
the active colour, the tool sizes, the window type and position. Command line
arguments win over the saved values. Removing the file restores the defaults.
.SH CLIPBOARD
After the editor closes, the picture in the clipboard is held by a forked
process of
.B xsnip
that owns the X11 selection and answers requests for
.BR image/png
(with the INCR protocol for large images). This is required because an X11
selection only exists while its owner runs; the helper exits when another
program takes the selection over.
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 on success; 1 if the shot was cancelled or the file was not written; 2 on
error (no display, bad arguments).
.SH EXAMPLES
Take a region and put it into the clipboard:
.PP
.nf
xsnip -s
.fi
.PP
Take the whole virtual screen and save it:
.PP
.nf
xsnip -a -o /tmp/screen.png
.fi
.PP
A floating window in the bottom right corner with a green pen:
.PP
.nf
xsnip -s --window dialog --pos br --color 2
.fi
.SH NOTES
X11 only: under Wayland grabbing the root window is not possible. Monitors are
detected through the Xinerama extension.
.SH AUTHOR
Dmitry Oboukhov <unera@debian.org>
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR scrot (1),
.BR xclip (1),
.BR import (1)
